OPEN.push(
{t:"ni",pts:12,q:"'Housing is becoming unaffordable.' Define the problem and a likely solution from (a) a historical institutionalist, (b) a sociological institutionalist and (c) an evolutionary institutionalist perspective. (Lecture 29 Sept homework)",
model:"(a) HI: unaffordability results from path-dependent institutions (e.g. mortgage-interest deduction, land and tenure rules) reproduced by positive feedback and the interests they create; change needs a critical juncture (a crisis as permissive condition) plus productive conditions: coalitions ready with an alternative (Sorensen). (b) SI: the problem is embedded norms and meanings, homeownership as 'normal' and the legitimate template; change requires new meanings to be inculcated, through symbolic markers and new practices in under-institutionalised niches (e.g. cooperatives), not voluntaristic top-down design (Dembski & Salet). (c) Evolutionary: over-detailed, frequently changed, prescriptive rules block adaptation and supply; solution: modest, gradual, general and stable rules that allow discovery and incremental development (Moroni; Cozzolino).",
rubric:["HI: path dependency + critical juncture logic","SI: norms/meanings + embedding/symbols","Evolutionary: emergent institutions + modest general rules","Uses at least one author per perspective"]},
{t:"davoudi",pts:10,q:"Explain Davoudi's (2006) distinction between the instrumental and enlightenment models of evidence use and why she argues the instrumental view resembles 1960s–70s planning.",
model:"Instrumental: linear, direct research–policy link, either 'expert on top' (research leads) or 'expert on tap' (research serves policy demands), underpinned by three misconceptions (rational policy-making, only positive science counts, value-free experts who know best). Enlightenment: research indirectly informs understanding and debate, so evidence-informed rather than evidence-based. The resemblance: like systems/rational planning, it assumes planning is a science solving well-defined problems with value-free techniques and that complex political processes can be 'technicized, commanded and controlled'. In reality evidence competes with ideology, interests, institutions and prior information (Weiss).",
rubric:["Instrumental model incl. on top/on tap","Enlightenment model = evidence-informed","Three misconceptions or the four I's","Link to positivist/rational planning"]}
);

